ground RAG workflows only.\n4. **Performance scope is dev-experimentation** — no latency SLAs, no index-type selection, no corpus-size thresholds; the benchmark verifies same-order-of-magnitude vs the inline Milvus baseline with flat scan for typical small document sets.\n5. **Vector storage and application storage are independent** — only the vector_io provider (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) is configured; the application storage backend stays on its existing default. No SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration work.\n6. **Platform-shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives in RHOAI 3.6** — for the current release, Gen AI Studio must self-provision; provisioning must be designed for portability to the shared connection string.\n7. **Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164** (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) for operator-based self-provisioning, with an explicit go/no-go gate to be defined in sprint planning — modeled as the Investigation epic with conditional branches below.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning approach for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Configure the OGX pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground RAG | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based self-provisioning of PostgreSQL/pgvector for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation (branch) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B) | Implementation (branch) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001<br/>Investigation: provisioning go/no-go\"]\n    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E002<br/>pgvector vector_io provider wiring\"]\n    E004[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E004<br/>benchmark + resource sizing\"]\n    BA[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003<br/>operator-based provisioning (branch A)\"]\n    BB[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003<br/>BFF-managed provisioning (branch B)\"]\n    E001 --> BA\n    E001 --> BB\n    E002 --> E004\n```\n\nBranch epics are conditional: exactly one of BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 is executed, selected by the E001 outcome. They are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The go/no-go outcome determines whether the operator-based provisioning epic exists at all (true gate, action `remove` if the RHOAI-controlled operator layer does not land in time). |\n| E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Same gate selecting the fallback approach; the BFF-managed epic exists only if operator-based provisioning is not selected (action `remove`). |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 12 (consumer builds against producer's artifact) | The benchmark exercises the pgvector-backed playground RAG path that the provider-wiring epic delivers; it cannot run before that integration exists. |\n| No edge E001 → E002 | Step 5.5 (tiered delivery) / Rule 14 | The vector_io provider wiring consumes a PostgreSQL connection secret regardless of who provisions the database — identical deliverable under every branch outcome. It is Tier 1 independent work and must not be serialized behind the decision. |\n| No edge E002 ↔ BRANCH-A/B-E003 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Provider configuration and database provisioning touch different areas of the gen-ai BFF; overlap is a merge-coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n| No konflux-onboarding / license-validation / repo-onboarding epics | Rule 20 (not applicable) | No new component or container image is introduced — the pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL image already exists and is already referenced in the platform (Staff Engineer Correction 2), corroborated by the model-registry-operator architecture context. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLR table unavailable (see Input Health); rows are keyed to the strategy's acceptance criteria and Staff Engineer directives.\n\n| Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-------------|---------|-------|\n| AC-1 — default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning | BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Delivered by whichever provisioning branch the go/no-go selects. |\n| AC-2 — Gen AI Studio playground RAG functions with no loss of capability | E002 (delivers), E004 (verifies) | E004's benchmark is the \"no loss of capability\" measurement (order-of-magnitude, per Staff Engineer scope). |\n| AC-3 — Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | (descoped) | Removed per Staff Engineer Input: Auto Rag compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). |\n| AC-4 — vector store runs as a separate process, not inline | E002, BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Remote pgvector provider (E002) + separately deployed PostgreSQL instance (branch epics). |\n| AC-5 — multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation) | BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Per-namespace scoping per Staff Engineer preference; see Health Warnings. |\n| AC-6 — lightweight for resource-constrained environments | E004, BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Sizing seeded from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resources, adjusted by benchmark. |\n| Success — RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocked | E002 + selected branch epic | Inline-provider removal can proceed once the remote pgvector path is the playground default. |\n| SE — migration path to production-grade vector DB | E002 | Folded in as documented guidance (open question resolved by Staff Engineer: default store is for dev experimentation; production-scale users point at an external vector DB). |\n\nEvery available requirement is covered; no orphans.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR tables live in an unfetched attachment. Owner-team assignment relies on the strategy label `dashboard-crimson-scrum` and the Staff Engineer Input's framing of Gen AI Studio as the owning feature.\n- **AC-3 descoped:** Auto Rag removed from scope per Staff Engineer Input; customers should expect Auto Rag/pgvector compatibility to be handled in RHAISTRAT-7824. The strategy's Summary/Problem Statement still mention Auto Rag — the Staff Engineer Input takes precedence.\n- **Scoping open question resolved by direction, not decision:** the strategy asks per-namespace vs per-instance scoping. The Staff Engineer Input records per-namespace as preferred and removes the per-instance pressure by decoupling Auto Rag; branch epics therefore carry per-namespace scoping as a design constraint. Revisit if a per-instance consumer re-enters scope.\n- **Priority note on E004 (P1):** the benchmark epic verifies the P0 \"no loss of current capability\" acceptance criterion but does not deliver capability — the Staff Engineer Input scoped it as order-of-magnitude verification for a dev-experimentation default with no SLAs, so it is schedulable independently of the P0 chain. Flagged here so the inversion is a recorded decision, not an oversight.\n- **Release-timing risk (schedule, not AC):** the Staff Engineer Input states it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a specific release until the operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is delivered. The last-resort fallback — customers bringing their own PostgreSQL via OGXServer configuration — degrades the out-of-the-box acceptance criterion and is recorded as gate-failure fallback text on both branch epics, not as an epic.\n- **Passive component — OGX distribution:** the ogx-distribution already ships the remote pgvector vector_io provider (corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`); no OGX code change is required. Its validation is carried as acceptance criteria on E002/E004 rather than a separate epic (same owning team).\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nAll epics use the canonical component `Gen AI Studio` (from `.context/rhai-components.txt`). The work lives in the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF (provider wiring, BFF-managed provisioning) and, under branch A, in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer that the dashboard operator modularization provides for Gen AI Studio — the single most-relevant canonical name for all of it is `Gen AI Studio`. The OGX distribution is passive (see Health Warnings). Auto Rag was removed from affected components per Staff Engineer Input.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency with uncertain timing — RHAISTRAT-7164 delivering a RHOAI-controlled operator — admits partial value without it:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E001 (decision gate) and E002 (pgvector vector_io provider wiring, deliverable under every outcome). Partial value: the remote-pgvector RAG path is functional against any reachable pgvector instance, decoupling Gen AI Studio from inline providers.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** the provisioning branch epics (BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003), selected by the E001 outcome; BRANCH-B is precisely the fallback that preserves Tier-1 value if the operator dependency slips.\n\nNo linear blocking of all work on RHAISTRAT-7164 — the conditional-branch structure plus branch-independent E002 is the tiered split.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\n- **Branch A (`RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003`)** — outcome: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer lands in time. Operator-based PostgreSQL self-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern. Gate failure action: `remove`.\n- **Branch B (`RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003`)** — outcome: operator layer does not land in time (or is not selected). BFF-managed provisioning mirroring the existing guardrails-infrastructure pattern. Gate failure action: `remove`.\n\nBoth branch files set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, include the gating Investigation in `dependencies`, and carry `gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic.\n\n## Epic Count and Critical Path\n\n`epic_count` counts all epics including branches: 5 (3 main-plan + 2 branch). Main-plan critical path length: 2 (E002 → E004); branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path per convention, and the gate chain E001 → branch epic is also length 2.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":58947,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aebb304c45787a7b","agent_id":"abc2c9b5f097a6743","session_id":"aa08db75-5b60-4f93-a4ef-03fcf69423a1","uuid":"b6564c05-8cf5-481c-82de-ef9e686d0676","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:32:20.309Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
