{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-4aefddc6-9763-42f4-a404-7ad9ee57abd2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba1a7de265420b75","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-tew96lw_/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning approach for the Gen AI Studio default vector store\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic resolves the central open decision in the strategy: how the default pgvector-backed vector store's PostgreSQL database is provisioned for Gen AI Studio in the current release. Operator-based self-provisioning requires a RHOAI-controlled operator for Gen AI Studio, which the dashboard operator modularization effort (RHAISTRAT-7164, In Progress) is expected to provide; if that does not land in time for the target release, the fallback is BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF. The Staff Engineer Input directs the development team to define a go/no-go gate for this decision as part of sprint planning. The outcome selects which provisioning implementation is executed and is recorded as a decision document.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Assess the delivery readiness and timeline of the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) against the target release dates.\n- Define the go/no-go gate criteria and decision date in sprint planning, tied to the release milestone.\n- Evaluate the two viable provisioning approaches against the strategy's requirements:\n  - Out-of-the-box experience: no manual user provisioning or configuration.\n  - Lifecycle management: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, credential rotation (a BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so these need explicit design attention).\n  - Portability to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL expected in the following release (consume a shared connection string rather than self-provision).\n  - Per-namespace isolation for multi-tenant scoping.\n- Record the selected approach, the evidence, and the impact of each outcome on the out-of-the-box requirement.\n- Document the last-resort fallback — customers supplying their own PostgreSQL by configuring the OGXServer custom resource — including its user-facing impact, in case neither self-provisioning path is viable.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written decision record identifies the selected provisioning approach and cites the evidence used (operator modularization delivery status, lifecycle-management trade-off analysis).\n- Go/no-go gate criteria and a decision date are defined and agreed in sprint planning, anchored to the target release.\n- The decision record documents, for each outcome, the impact on the out-of-the-box experience requirement.\n- The last-resort fallback (customer-supplied PostgreSQL via OGXServer configuration) is documented with its user-facing impact.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Gates AC-1 (out-of-the-box default vector store) and AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping): the decision determines how per-namespace provisioning is delivered.\n- Staff Engineer directives: hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 for operator-based self-provisioning; go/no-go gate to be defined in sprint planning; fallback paths (BFF-managed provisioning, customer-supplied PostgreSQL).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":65329,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aebb304c45787a7b","agent_id":"abc2c9b5f097a6743","session_id":"aa08db75-5b60-4f93-a4ef-03fcf69423a1","uuid":"fa124158-e52e-4546-bfef-ba1098a6b22f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:32:34.078Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
