{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-db8ee187-5887-477e-86dc-4b830b17b384","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a55b82ae8b80096c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-tew96lw_/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B)\n\n## Description\n\nBranch outcome B, executed if the go/no-go decision selects the fallback because the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (from the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for the target release. The gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable database exists in the namespace — mirroring how the BFF already provisions guardrails infrastructure. It uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector included (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); no custom image build or pipeline onboarding is needed. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management receives explicit design attention.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement idempotent provisioning in the gen-ai BFF: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, persistent storage, and credentials secret; reuse an existing instance when one is present.\n- Wire the provisioned database's connection details into the OGX vector_io provider configuration the BFF already produces.\n- Design and implement lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation each get an explicit, tested design.\n- Seed PostgreSQL resource requests/limits from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point.\n- Keep the image reference resolvable for disconnected installs, reusing the platform's existing related-image mechanism for the PostgreSQL 16 image.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (following release), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A playground with RAG configured in a namespace has a running, pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance provisioned without any user action beyond enabling the playground.\n- Provisioning is idempotent: reconfiguring or restarting the playground does not duplicate or destroy the database; an existing suitable instance is reused.\n- The provisioned database's connection details reach the OGX vector_io provider via a secret, and playground RAG workflows succeed end to end against it.\n- Lifecycle behaviors are explicitly designed and tested: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery on pod restarts, and credential rotation behave as documented.\n- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference sizing, with any adjustments justified.\n- The image reference is overridable for disconnected installs through the platform's existing related-image mechanism.\n- The design documents the migration path to consuming the platform-provided shared database connection string when it becomes available.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-1 — a default vector store is available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure.\n- AC-4 — the vector store runs as a separate process (a standalone PostgreSQL deployment), decoupled from the OGX instance.\n- AC-5 — scoped for multi-tenant environments via per-namespace isolation.\n- AC-6 — lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments (sizing seeded from the reference operator).\n- Staff Engineer directives: alternative fallback of BFF-managed provisioning modeled on the guardrails infrastructure pattern; portability to the shared platform database; explicit lifecycle design because the BFF has no reconciliation loop; existing RH PostgreSQL 16 image with pgvector.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":66905,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aebb304c45787a7b","agent_id":"abc2c9b5f097a6743","session_id":"aa08db75-5b60-4f93-a4ef-03fcf69423a1","uuid":"04c025fa-8e79-42b2-b210-f9ab77897e7d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:33:02.917Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
