{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9efdc96361934a6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B)\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: remove\n15\t  fallback_approach: If operator-based provisioning is selected by the go/no-go decision,\n16\t    this epic is removed; if neither self-provisioning path is viable, customers supply\n17\t    their own PostgreSQL by configuring the OGXServer custom resource, which degrades\n18\t    the out-of-the-box experience and is aligned with the breaking changes of OGX\n19\t    1.0.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: 0\n26\t  external_dependency: 1\n27\t  human_process_gates: 1\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 8\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\t---\n36\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B)\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tBranch outcome B, executed if the go/no-go decision selects the fallback because the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (from the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for the target release. The gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable database exists in the namespace — mirroring how the BFF already provisions guardrails infrastructure. It uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector included (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); no custom image build or pipeline onboarding is needed. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management receives explicit design attention.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Implement idempotent provisioning in the gen-ai BFF: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, persistent storage, and credentials secret; reuse an existing instance when one is present.\n45\t- Wire the provisioned database's connection details into the OGX vector_io provider configuration the BFF already produces.\n46\t- Design and implement lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation each get an explicit, tested design.\n47\t- Seed PostgreSQL resource requests/limits from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point.\n48\t- Keep the image reference resolvable for disconnected installs, reusing the platform's existing related-image mechanism for the PostgreSQL 16 image.\n49\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (following release), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- A playground with RAG configured in a namespace has a running, pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance provisioned without any user action beyond enabling the playground.\n54\t- Provisioning is idempotent: reconfiguring or restarting the playground does not duplicate or destroy the database; an existing suitable instance is reused.\n55\t- The provisioned database's connection details reach the OGX vector_io provider via a secret, and playground RAG workflows succeed end to end against it.\n56\t- Lifecycle behaviors are explicitly designed and tested: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery on pod restarts, and credential rotation behave as documented.\n57\t- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference sizing, with any adjustments justified.\n58\t- The image reference is overridable for disconnected installs through the platform's existing related-image mechanism.\n59\t- The design documents the migration path to consuming the platform-provided shared database connection string when it becomes available.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- AC-1 — a default vector store is available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure.\n64\t- AC-4 — the vector store runs as a separate process (a standalone PostgreSQL deployment), decoupled from the OGX instance.\n65\t- AC-5 — scoped for multi-tenant environments via per-namespace isolation.\n66\t- AC-6 — lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments (sizing seeded from the reference operator).\n67\t- Staff Engineer directives: alternative fallback of BFF-managed provisioning modeled on the guardrails infrastructure pattern; portability to the shared platform database; explicit lifecycle design because the BFF has no reconciliation loop; existing RH PostgreSQL 16 image with pgvector.\n68\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4690990295981d0","agent_id":"ad43b990017bf9d40","session_id":"aa08db75-5b60-4f93-a4ef-03fcf69423a1","uuid":"08dbff3f-4eca-4f7e-be38-fa94f2d04f79","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:35:00.868Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9efdc96361934a6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
