nce, scan results: the consuming epic gets an AC (\"the evidence package includes the signed attestations produced by the build pipeline\"), not an edge. Code builds against an API, CRD, or library; it does not build against attestations or digests.\n138:### Epic Generation Rules\n144:### Acceptance Criteria Rules\n242:- Rules 23-25 from Step 5\n330:Add optional fields only when non-null: `implementation_type=<value>`, `branch=<value>`, `gated_by=<epic_id>`, `gate_failure_impact.action=<value> gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"<text>\"`. Every epic that depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that Investigation (see Rule 3).\n349:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0), implementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10 — no `dependencies` edge, in either direction, between `license-validation` and `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation` and `konflux-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` and a general implementation; a flagged edge is removed from the dependent epic's `dependencies` and its coupling expressed as an AC gate instead), and shared-surface enforcement deliverables (a standalone enforcement-tool epic — lint rule, CI check, static-analysis check, validation harness — over surfaces implemented by same-component epics; the deliverable is divided per surface into the consuming epics' Scope/ACs and the standalone epic deleted). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a48e6c1126e1b360","agent_id":"a2328aea6b3a75f23","s