03 and BRANCH-B-E003, and a main-plan epic cannot (correctly) depend on a branch epic. So the DAG as drawn lets E004 start as soon as E002 lands, with no producer of the database it benchmarks and no statement in E004's scope that it stands up its own instance. The decomposition's own justification for the E002 → E004 edge (\"it cannot run before that integration exists\") proves the decomposer reasoned about exactly this path — but the integration includes a reachable database, which E002 does not deliver.\n  - Correction (either resolves it): (a) add an explicit scope item + AC to E004 stating the benchmark self-provisions a standalone benchmark PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (seeded from the same reference sizing) rather than consuming the provisioned default — this matches the Staff Engineer's \"sprint 1 benchmark\" framing, which predates any provisioning completion; or (b) if the benchmark must exercise the provisioned default path, gate E004 on E001 (`gated_by` + dependency) and state in Scope that the benchmarked instance is the one provisioned by the selected branch — never a `dependencies` edge to the branch epic itself.\n\nA minor citation imprecision (not scored): the E002 → E004 edge is justified as \"Rule 12 (consumer builds against producer's artifact)\" — Rule 12 covers framework/library → consumer; the applicable rule is Rule 13 (artifact consumption). The edge itself is valid under either reading.\n\nBecause a critical issue forces this criterion to 0, and any criterion at 0 forces `pass: false`, the review fails despite the otherwise high total.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nAll five epics are team `dashboard-crimson-scrum`, component `Gen AI Studio` (canonical, verified in `.context/rhai-components.txt`). Branch A's work lives in the operator layer delivered by RHAISTRAT-7164 rather than the BFF — the Component Mapping section addresses this explicitly and the canonical name is the best available; acceptable. No epic exceeds ~2 weeks (provider wiring; go/no-go decision record; benchmark; one provisioning implementation each). No heterogeneous monoliths. No scope duplication: the two branch epics are mutually exclusive alternatives selected by a gate (exactly one executes), not sibling rebuilds of the same surface; E002 (provider config) and the branches (database provisioning) touch different areas of the BFF per Rule 15, correctly kept parallel. E002's user-guidance documentation item is incidental documentation falling out of the code change (a config note, not a tutorial/notebook work stream) — correctly folded in as an AC rather than a standalone docs-authoring epic. No standalone enforcement-tool epic.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (operator layer lands in time or not) determines which of the two branch epics exists — a true existence gate with downstream dependents. Both branch epics carry `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact` populated (`action: remove` + a substantive `fallback_approach` naming the customer-supplied-PostgreSQL last resort) — nothing is a bare scheduling dependency. Every `gated_by` target (E001) appears in the corresponding epic's direct `dependencies`. E002, E004, and both branches are Implementations producing concrete artifacts (BFF configuration, benchmark report + sizing recommendation, provisioning implementations). E004 as Implementation is correct: it produces a deliverable (report, sizing recommendation) and its outcome does not change downstream structure — nothing depends on it, so it is not a misclassified Investigation.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal sets throughout: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); all four Implementation epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-contamination. All five ai-signals rationale files exist and their values match the frontmatter. Spot-checks: E001's `source_accessibility: 0` is correctly scored (the answer is a future de