{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89eb1970e7b93f3a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'E004 (benchmark) consumes a provisioned pgvector PostgreSQL instance\n10\t    through playground RAG workflows, but its only dependency E002 explicitly does\n11\t    not provision a database (its AC limits scope to beyond the database being reachable)\n12\t    and the only producers are the conditional branch epics, which a main-plan epic\n13\t    cannot depend on — a missing data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content.\n14\t    Fix: either add a scope item plus AC to E004 stating the benchmark self-provisions\n15\t    a standalone benchmark PostgreSQL/pgvector instance seeded from the reference\n16\t    sizing (matches the Staff Engineer sprint-1 framing), or gate E004 on E001 (gated_by\n17\t    plus dependency) and state that the benchmarked instance is the one provisioned\n18\t    by the selected branch — never a dependencies edge to the branch epic.'\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n21\t  description: DAG Justification cites Rule 12 (framework/library to consumer) for\n22\t    the E002-E004 edge; the applicable rule is Rule 13 (artifact consumption). Edge\n23\t    is valid under either reading; fix the citation.\n24\terror: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8004\n27\t\n28\t## Review Summary\n29\t\n30\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n31\tRecommendation: revise\n32\t\n33\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and shared-surface checks all passed).\n34\t\n35\tTriage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition); 5 epics (3 main-plan + 2 conditional branches) is the expected shape, no triage contradiction.\n36\t\n37\tThe decomposition is strong on six of seven dimensions. It fails on one structural DAG defect: the benchmark epic (E004) consumes an artifact (a provisioned, reachable pgvector PostgreSQL instance) that no epic it depends on produces — the only producers are the conditional branch epics, which a main-plan epic cannot depend on, and the epic's scope never states that it provisions its own benchmark instance. Per the auto-fail rule (any criterion at 0 → `pass: false`), the decomposition requires revision before acceptance.\n38\t\n39\t## Criterion Details\n40\t\n41\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n42\tThe strategy's HLR table lives in an unfetched Jira attachment; the decomposition states this transparently (Input Health) and keys traceability to the strategy's acceptance criteria AC-1…AC-6 plus Staff Engineer directives. Every available requirement maps to at least one epic: AC-1/AC-5 to the provisioning branches, AC-2 to E002 (deliver) + E004 (verify), AC-4 to E002 + branches, AC-6 to E004 + branches. AC-3 (Auto Rag) is descoped — explicitly directed by the Staff Engineer Input (\"Remove Auto Rag from affected components… acceptance criteria\"), with the rationale recorded in Health Warnings; this is a justified deferral, not a gap. Priority inheritance is clean: every epic that blocks P0 work (E001, E002, both branches) is itself P0; E004 (P1) blocks nothing. The E004-at-P1 inversion (a P1 epic verifying a P0 acceptance criterion) is a recorded decision in Health Warnings with the Staff Engineer's dev-experimentation scoping as justification — acceptable. No priority collapse: the only multi-priority surface (deliver vs. verify AC-2) is split into E002/E004, which is deferrable independently.\n43\t\n44\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n45\tDiagram/frontmatter consistency is correct: every edge in the mermaid DAG (`E001 → BRANCH-A-E003`, `E001 → BRANCH-B-E003`, `E002 → E004`) has a matching `dependencies` entry, and vice versa; arrows point dependency → dependent. Both branch files set `branch` (A/B, matching filenames), `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, include E001 in `dependencies`, and carry `gate_failure_impact` with action and fallback. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies. Critical path length 2 is reasonable for a tiered medium strategy. No konflux/license/repo-onboarding epics — correct per Staff Engineer Correction 2 (the pgvector image already exists and is referenced in the platform), so Rules 7/8/10 are vacuous.\n46\t\n47\tHowever, there is one **critical** issue — a missing data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content:\n48\t\n49\t- **E004 consumes a provisioned PostgreSQL instance that nothing it depends on produces.** E004's scope is to \"run a reproducible benchmark comparing the inline Milvus provider baseline against the remote pgvector provider through playground RAG workflows,\" and its sizing AC targets \"the provisioned PostgreSQL instance.\" E002 — E004's only dependency — explicitly does not provision a database: its own AC limits E002's deliverable to \"no manual provider setup… beyond the database being reachable.\" The only epics that produce a provisioned, per-namespace pgvector instance are BRANCH-A-E003 and BRANCH-B-E003, and a main-plan epic cannot (correctly) depend on a branch epic. So the DAG as drawn lets E004 start as soon as E002 lands, with no producer of the database it benchmarks and no statement in E004's scope that it stands up its own instance. The decomposition's own justification for the E002 → E004 edge (\"it cannot run before that integration exists\") proves the decomposer reasoned about exactly this path — but the integration includes a reachable database, which E002 does not deliver.\n50\t  - Correction (either resolves it): (a) add an explicit scope item + AC to E004 stating the benchmark self-provisions a standalone benchmark PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (seeded from the same reference sizing) rather than consuming the provisioned default