y-operator.md confirms controller-created `{registry-name}-postgres` deployments and the sizing reference; ogx-k8s-operator.md confirms the upstream sync model. All cited claims are corroborated; no calibration misses.\n64\t\n65\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n66\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy and Staff Engineer directives. E002, as the replacement for the inline-provider default, carries the rule-mandated reversibility/rollback AC (revert to the inline provider via the `ENABLE_PGVECTOR` guard while inline providers still exist) — well grounded in the RHAISTRAT-7474 sequencing. No konflux or docs-authoring epics exist, so Rules 25/24 (build-pipeline-green, doc technical review) are not applicable. No externally-controlled preconditions misfiled as ACs — the release-timing risk and customer-supplied-PostgreSQL fallback are correctly recorded as gate-failure fallback text and Health Warnings, not ACs. No draft-ID references in any epic body (grep-verified; only frontmatter epic_ids and real Jira keys appear).\n67\t\n68\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n69\tAll in-scope strategy content is accounted for: AC-1/2/4/5/6 covered (see Criterion 1); AC-3 descoped per explicit Staff Engineer directive with the owning ticket (RHAISTRAT-7824) named. The Success Criteria are carried (RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocking noted in the traceability matrix; no-regression/no-manual-setup covered by E002 + branches). Both strategy Open Questions are resolved and carried: scoping model (per-namespace, with the revisit trigger recorded in Health Warnings) and migration path (folded into E002 as documented guidance). Staff Engineer corrections 1-3 and all directives (portability design, lifecycle attention, disconnected installs, sizing reference, sprint-1 benchmark scope) appear in the relevant epics' Scope/ACs. Conditional branches cover the bounded outcome space (operator lands / doesn't land, plus the documented last-resort fallback as gate-failure text rather than 