{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4815b132ee59fa5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n10\t\n11\t## Input Health\n12\t\n13\tThe full strategy body was stored as a Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in the workspace. The Affected Components, Impacted Teams, High Level Requirements, Dependencies, and Non-Functional Requirements tables were therefore unavailable. The decomposition is derived from the Summary, Problem Statement, Acceptance Criteria, Open Questions, and the Staff Engineer Input (which takes precedence per pipeline rules). HLR traceability below is keyed to the strategy's acceptance criteria (AC-1 … AC-6) and Staff Engineer directives.\n14\t\n15\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (traceability)\n16\t\n17\tThe Staff Engineer Input materially redirected this decomposition. Logged overrides:\n18\t\n19\t1. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — OGX defaults to SQLite for application storage; the strategy's premise that a PostgreSQL database already exists per distribution instance is incorrect. Introducing PostgreSQL is new work (the provisioning branch epics below).\n20\t2. **A Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector already exists** (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`) and is already referenced by the model-registry-operator and present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs. The strategy's 1-2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated — no image build epic, no Konflux onboarding epic, no license-validation epic.\n21\t3. **Auto Rag is decoupled** — Auto Rag compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). AC-3 is descoped from this strategy; validation covers Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows only.\n22\t4. **Performance scope is dev-experimentation** — no latency SLAs, no index-type selection, no corpus-size thresholds; the benchmark verifies same-order-of-magnitude vs the inline Milvus baseline with flat scan for typical small document sets.\n23\t5. **Vector storage and application storage are independent** — only the vector_io provider (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) is configured; the application storage backend stays on its existing default. No SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration work.\n24\t6. **Platform-shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives in RHOAI 3.6** — for the current release, Gen AI Studio must self-provision; provisioning must be designed for portability to the shared connection string.\n25\t7. **Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164** (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) for operator-based self-provisioning, with an explicit go/no-go gate to be defined in sprint planning — modeled as the Investigation epic with conditional branches below.\n26\t\n27\t## Epic List\n28\t\n29\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n30\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning approach for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Configure the OGX pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground RAG | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based self-provisioning of PostgreSQL/pgvector for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation (branch) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B) | Implementation (branch) | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P1 |\n36\t\n37\t## Dependency DAG\n38\t\n39\t```mermaid\n40\tgraph TD\n41\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001<br/>Investigation: provisioning go/no-go\"]\n42\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E002<br/>pgvector vector_io provider wiring\"]\n43\t    E004[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E004<br/>benchmark + resource sizing\"]\n44\t    BA[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003<br/>operator-based provisioning (branch A)\"]\n45\t    BB[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003<br/>BFF-managed provisioning (branch B)\"]\n46\t    E001 --> BA\n47\t    E001 --> BB\n48\t    E002 --> E004\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tBranch epics are conditional: exactly one of BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 is executed, selected by the E001 outcome. They are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n52\t\n53\t## DAG Justification\n54\t\n55\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n56\t|------|------|-----------|\n57\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The go/no-go outcome determines whether the operator-based provisioning epic exists at all (true gate, action `remove` if the RHOAI-controlled operator layer does not land in time). |\n58\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Same gate selecting the fallback approach; the BFF-managed epic exists only if operator-based provisioning is not selected (action `remove`). |\n59\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12 (consumer builds against producer's artifact) | The benchmark exercises the pgvector-backed playground RAG path that the provider-wiring epic delivers; it cannot run before that integration exists. |\n60\t| No edge E001 → E002 | Step 5.5 (tiered delivery) / Rule 14 | The vector_io provider wiring consumes a PostgreSQL connection secret regardless of who provisions the database — identical deliverable under every branch outcome. It is Tier 1 independent work and must not be serialized behind the decision. |\n61\t| No edge E002 ↔ BRANCH-A/B-E003 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Provider configuration and database provisioning touch different areas of the gen-ai BFF; overlap is a merge-coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n62\t| No konflux-onboarding / license-validation / repo-onboarding epics | Rule 20 (not applicable) | No new component or container image is introduced — the pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL image already exists and is already referenced in the platform (Staff Engineer Correction 2), corroborated by the model-registry-operator architecture context. |\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n65\t\n66\tHLR table unavailable (see Input Health); rows are keyed to the strategy's acceptance criteria and Staff Engineer directives.\n67\t\n68\t| Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n69\t|-------------|---------|-------|\n70\t| AC-1 — default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning | BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Delivered by whichever provisioning branch the go/no-go selects. |\n71\t| AC-2 — Gen AI Studio playground RAG functions with no loss of capability | E002 (delivers), E004 (verifies) | E004's benchmark is the \"no loss of capability\" measurement (order-of-magnitude, per Staff Engineer scope). |\n72\t| AC-3 — Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | (descoped) | Removed per Staff Engineer Input: Auto Rag compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). |\n73\t| AC-4 — vector store runs as a separate process, not inline | E002, BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Remote pgvector provider (E002) + separately deployed PostgreSQL instance (branch epics). |\n74\t| AC-5 — multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation) | BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Per-namespace scoping per Staff Engineer preference; see Health Warnings. |\n75\t| AC-6 — lightweight for resource-constrained environments | E004, BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Sizing seeded from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resources, adjusted by benchmark. |\n76\t| Success — RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocked | E002 + selected branch epic | Inline-provider removal can proceed once the remote pgvector path is the playground default. |\n77\t| SE — migration path to production-grade vector DB | E002 | Folded in as documented guidance (open question resolved by Staff Engineer: default store is for dev experimentation; production-scale users point at an external vector DB). |\n78\t\n79\tEvery available requirement is covered; no orphans.\n80\t\n81\t## Health Warnings\n82\t\n83\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR tables live in an unfetched attachment. Owner-team assignment relies on the strategy label `dashboard-crimson-scrum` and the Staff Engineer Input's framing of Gen AI Studio as the owning feature.\n84\t- **AC-3 descoped:** Auto Rag removed from scope per Staff Engineer Input; customers should expect Auto Rag/pgvector compatibility to be handled in RHAISTRAT-7824. The strategy's Summary/Problem Statement still mention Auto Rag — the Staff Engineer Input takes precedence.\n85\t- **Scoping open question resolved by direction, not decision:** the strategy asks per-namespace vs per-instance scoping. The Staff Engineer Input records per-namespace as preferred and removes the per-instance pressure by decoupling Auto Rag; branch epics therefore carry per-namespace scoping as a design constraint. Revisit if a per-instance consumer re-enters scope.\n86\t- **Priority note on E004 (P1):** the benchmark epic verifies the P0 \"no loss of current capability\" acceptance criterion but does not deliver capability — the Staff Engineer Input scoped it as order-of-magnitude verification for a dev-experimentation default with no SLAs, so it is schedulable independently of the P0 chain. Flagged here so the inversion is a recorded decision, not an oversight.\n87\t- **Release-timing risk (schedule, not AC):** the Staff Engineer Input states it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a specific release until the operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is delivered. The last-resort fallback — customers bringing their own PostgreSQL via OGXServer configuration — degrades the out-of-the-box acceptance criterion and is recorded as gate-failure fallback text on both branch epics, not as an epic.\n88\t- **Passive component — OGX distribution:** the ogx-distribution already ships the remote pgvector vector_io provider (corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`); no OGX code change is required. Its validation is carried as acceptance criteria on E002/E004 rather than a separate epic (same owning team).\n89\t\n90\t## Component Mapping\n91\t\n92\tAll epics use the canonical component `Gen AI Studio` (from `.context/rhai-components.txt`). The work lives in the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF (provider wiring, BFF-managed provisioning) and, under branch A, in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer that the dashboard operator modularization provides for Gen AI Studio — the single most-relevant canonical name for all of it is `Gen AI Studio`. The OGX distribution is passive (see Health Warnings). Auto Rag was removed from affected components per Staff Engineer Input.\n93\t\n94\t## Tiered Delivery\n95\t\n96\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing — RHAISTRAT-7164 delivering a RHOAI-controlled operator — admits partial value without it:\n97\t\n98\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E001 (decision gate) and E002 (pgvector vector_io provider wiring, deliverable under every outcome). Partial value: the remote-pgvector RAG path is functional against any reachable pgvector instance, decoupling Gen AI Studio from inline providers.\n99\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** the provisioning branch epics (BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003), selected by the E001 outcome; BRANCH-B is precisely the fallback that preserves Tier-1 value if the operator dependency slips.\n100\t\n101\tNo linear blocking of all work on RHAISTRAT-7164 — the conditional-branch structure plus branch-independent E002 is the tiered split.\n102\t\n103\t## Conditional Branches\n104\t\n105\t- **Branch A (`RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003`)** — outcome: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer lands in time. Operator-based PostgreSQL self-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern. Gate failure action: `remove`.\n106\t- **Branch B (`RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003`)** — outcome: operator layer does not land in time (or is not selected). BFF-managed provisioning mirroring the existing guardrails-infrastructure pattern. Gate failure action: `remove`.\n107\t\n108\tBoth branch files set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, include the gating Investigation in `dependencies`, and carry `gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic.\n109\t\n110\t## Epic Count and Critical Path\n111\t\n112\t`epic_count` counts all epics including branches: 5 (3 main-plan + 2 branch). Main-plan critical path length: 2 (E002 → E004); branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path per convention, and the gate chain E001 → branch epic is also length 2.\n113\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e11c1839da5dbb1","agent_id":"a3aa13f69618cd4f5","session_id":"aa08db75-5b60-4f93-a4ef-03fcf69423a1","uuid":"4f52ba0b-90fd-4bec-a798-2acea57bcd05","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:44:29.620Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4815b132ee59fa5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Second revise RHAISTRAT-8004"}
