{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b6bd7df3a02f0d5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E004\n3\ttitle: Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource\n4\t  sizing\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P1\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n12\tai_signals:\n13\t  change_specificity: 1\n14\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n15\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n16\t  existing_foundation: 0\n17\t  open_questions: 1\n18\t  external_dependency: 1\n19\t  human_process_gates: 1\n20\t  repo_access: 1\n21\t  architecture_claims: 1\n22\timplementation_type: null\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 6\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tThis epic delivers the sprint-1 benchmark the Staff Engineer Input calls for: capture the inline Milvus provider's baseline performance and verify that pgvector is in the same order of magnitude for typical Gen AI Studio playground RAG usage, then derive the PostgreSQL resource sizing for the default vector store. The default store is intended for development experimentation — strict latency SLAs are explicitly not required; the pass condition is order-of-magnitude parity, not a threshold. Typical playground RAG usage involves small document sets, for which a flat scan (no vector index) is sufficient.\n36\t\n37\t## Acceptance Criteria\n38\t\n39\t- A benchmark report records the inline Milvus baseline and the pgvector results for representative playground RAG workloads (small document sets, flat scan, insert and query paths).\n40\t- The benchmark runs against a standalone, self-provisioned pgvector PostgreSQL instance (existing platform-referenced image, seeded from the model-registry-operator's reference sizing); the benchmark does not depend on the default-provisioning path being delivered, and its setup is scripted and reproducible.\n41\t- Pgvector results are within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline; deviations beyond that are escalated as findings rather than silently accepted.\n42\t- A resource sizing recommendation for the provisioned PostgreSQL instance is documented, seeded from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits, with any adjustments justified by the benchmark results.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- Define and run a reproducible benchmark comparing the inline Milvus provider baseline against the remote pgvector provider through playground RAG workflows (document ingestion and retrieval).\n47\t- Use representative dev-experimentation workloads: small document sets, flat scan, no vector index selection, no corpus-size threshold studies.\n48\t- Capture the inline Milvus baseline before the inline provider is removed from the distribution, so the comparison is grounded.\n49\t- Self-provision a standalone benchmark PostgreSQL/pgvector instance — using the existing pgvector-enabled image already referenced by the platform (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`), seeded from the model-registry-operator's reference sizing — rather than consuming the provisioned default. The benchmark is a sprint-1 deliverable and must be runnable before any default-provisioning path lands; the provisioning approach is selected independently and later.\n50\t- Produce the benchmark report and the PostgreSQL resource sizing recommendation (requests/limits), seeded from the model-registry-operator's sizing.\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability\n53\t\n54\t- AC-2 — playground RAG functions with no loss of current capability: this epic is the measured verification of that criterion (order-of-magnitude parity with the inline baseline).\n55\t- AC-6 — the default vector store is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments: verified through the resource sizing recommendation.\n56\t- Staff Engineer directives: performance and index thresholds are dev-experimentation scope (no SLAs, order-of-magnitude check, flat scan); resource sizing references the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL settings.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a83b4a1fcd8be7d","agent_id":"a5caa00a7e8404331","session_id":"aa08db75-5b60-4f93-a4ef-03fcf69423a1","uuid":"a225a22b-80b3-4695-a084-017c0808180c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:45:40.642Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b6bd7df3a02f0d5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Final re-review RHAISTRAT-8004"}
